    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/channels/detecting-fraudulent-job-postings/": {
      "title": "Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings",
      "description": "Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Fraudulent employment and internship opportunities are everywhere. Unfortunately, college students are a big target, as they are often under considerable financial pressure to find work. This is especially true for those who are: Young and inexperienced International and undocumented students Non-native English-speakers Returnees to the workforce With recent advances in technology, such as AI/ChatGPT and improved Virtual Private Networks, scammers are better equipped than ever, and some of the old markers of a scam opportunity are becoming less relevant. They may still apply, but an opportunity may have none of these markers and still be a scam. In addition, scammers may be found all over the world, including the United States, and across all demographics. View the essential information below and the resources on this page to learn how to spot potential scams, how to prevent yourself from being scammed, and what to do if you believe someone has scammed you. How do I know if a job is a scam? One of the best ways to avoid being scammed is to know what to look for. Get familiar with the common signs so you can quickly identify a potentially fraudulent opportunity. Signs an Opportunity Might Be a Scam What should I do if I think a job I’m looking at is a scam? As you job search, you may come across opportunities that have signs of being a scam. If you think something might be a scam, know what to do before you apply or engage with them further. Steps To Take if You Think It’s a Scam I think I’ve been scammed. What do I do now? If you think you’ve been scammed the most important think to do is to take immediate action. Act quickly. This can help to lessen the damage. Completely break off contact with the scammer: Block phone numbers, email addresses, and social media accounts and report them to their respective service providers. Forward any written correspondence and electronic records (e.g., phone logs) to Rutgers IT ( abuse@rutgers.edu ). Get your electronic devices—laptop, tablet, phone, etc.—checked for spyware or malware. Contact Rutgers police and file a report. A police report is usually required by financial institutions as documentation that fraud took place. File a report with the Federal Trade Commission ( FTC.gov ). It investigates large-scale fraud, and you are likely one of thousands. Next Steps if You’ve Been Scammed Latest News How to Spot a Scam During Your Job Search Do Not Apply – Spot a Social Media Job Scam Fraudulent Employers: Tips for Career Centers and Students Safety: Best Practices for Job Searches Job Scams Are on the Rise—Here’s How to Spot Them and Steer Clear View All Career Resources What To Do if You’ve Been Scammed Share This: Share What To Do if You’ve Been Scammed on Facebook Share What To Do if You’ve Been Scammed on LinkedIn Share What To Do if You’ve Been Scammed on X Copy Link Think you’ve been scammed? The most important thing to do is to take action immediately. Read our steps below so … Read More Signs an Opportunity Might Be a Scam Share This: Share Signs an Opportunity Might Be a Scam on Facebook Share Signs an Opportunity Might Be a Scam on LinkedIn Share Signs an Opportunity Might Be a Scam on X Copy Link 🚩 8 red flags to watch for in your job search An Opportunity May Be a Scam if… 🚩 Information on the … Read More What To Do if You Think an Opportunity Is a Scam Share This: Share What To Do if You Think an Opportunity Is a Scam on Facebook Share What To Do if You Think an Opportunity Is a Scam on LinkedIn Share What To Do if You Think an Opportunity Is a Scam on X Copy Link If you’re not sure if an opportunity is a scam or not, here’s our advice to help you avoid a … Read More Statement on Addresses Share This: Share Statement on Addresses on Facebook Share Statement on Addresses on LinkedIn Share Statement on Addresses on X Copy Link View Resource Federal Trade Commission: Job Scams Share This: Share Federal Trade Commission: Job Scams on Facebook Share Federal Trade Commission: Job Scams on LinkedIn Share Federal Trade Commission: Job Scams on X Copy Link Read the Federal Trade Commission’s advice on job scams to make sure you can spot one right away and avoid … Read More Why you should leave your address off your resume Share This: Share Why you should leave your address off your resume on Facebook Share Why you should leave your address off your resume on LinkedIn Share Why you should leave your address off your resume on X Copy Link View Resource View All Resources Vault Guides Skip filter options to posts Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Resource Categories: Advising Guidelines Applications Career Guides Hiring Policies How-tos & Examples Industry Reports Professional Writing Samples Third Party Resources Vault Guides Close Clear Filters The Vault Guide to Resumes and Job-Hunting Skills, Second Edition Share This: Share The Vault Guide to Resumes and Job-Hunting Skills, Second Edition on Facebook Share The Vault Guide to Resumes and Job-Hunting Skills, Second Edition on LinkedIn Share The Vault Guide to Resumes and Job-Hunting Skills, Second Edition on X Copy Link It takes more than a solid résumé and cover letter to make yourself stand out to prospective employers. Networking and … Vault #9781438148465 Show author info A comprehensive resource for students and job seekers looking for career advice, job postings, company reviews from employees, and rankings of the best companies and industry employers. facebook instagram linkedin twitter Read More View All Resources Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
