Readiness Industry & Student Connections Career Operations & Strategic Initiatives Foundational Elements Core Values Vision and Mission Statements Leadership & Governance Advisory Boards & Councils Office of the Executive Director Outcomes Data CES Annual Reports National Trends and Predictions Rutgers Career Outcomes & Data Conference Toolkits Career Communities Toolkit RSSI Toolkit Policies and Guidelines CES Strategic Plan Join the CES Team Handshake Student Alumni Employer Career Advising Drop-In Advising Schedule Appointment Career & Internship Guide View All Articles Career Planning Networking Job Search Strategies Cover Letters, Email Etiquette, & Resume Writing Interview Techniques Job Offer Negotiations Graduate School Planning Events Full Calendar Workshops Employer Networking Information Sessions Career & Internship Fairs Career Conferences Visiting Recruiters Contact General Students Employers Campus Partners Alumni Technology Help Desk CES Staff Directory Students & Alumni Courses & Signature Programs Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship Program Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship Program Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship Program (NJ/NY/PA) Fostering Civic Engagement & Career Readiness Rutgers Scarlet Service establishes civic engagement as central to a Rutgers education—fostering appreciation of differences, restoring civil discourse, and advancing the common good. The Scarlet Service Initiative began with the Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program in 2022, run by the Rutgers–Camden Career Center, Rutgers–New Brunswick Office of Career Exploration and Success, and Rutgers–Newark Office of Career Resources and Exploration. Now supported by Eagleton’s Democracy Lab, the program continues to grow. Students, Apply Now! Student Information Recommend a Student Internship Site/Employer Information Refer an Internship Site/Employer Facts & Figures The Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship Program is not only shaping student experiences but also strengthening communities, and its growing impact is reflected in the numbers below. 100+ High-Impact Public Service Internships $5,000 Student Stipend 3 Funded Academic Credits RSSI is powered by the Rutgers Democracy Lab! Democracy isn't a spectator sport — it’s something you practice. The Rutgers Democracy Lab (RDL) creates opportunities for students to build civic skills, talk across differences, and work together to solve real-world problems. We collaborate with faculty, staff, and community partners to combine research on youth civic engagement, technology, and democracy with innovative programs that help students turn ideas into action. Our goal is simple: we mobilize the power of Rutgers to strengthen democracy. Learn More RSSI Goals & Benefits Our society has lost sight of the common good, a problem that threatens our future as a nation, our viability as a culture, and our humanity. As an institution of higher education, Rutgers has a vital imperative to improve the human condition and seek solutions for our most complex problems. There is no greater calling for university communities today than to make sure our students can pursue an excellent, life-changing education and engage in opportunities to better our world in fundamental ways. Goal #1: Provide an opportunity for Rutgers students to contribute to solutions of societal problems; view American life from a fresh perspective; and develop a better understanding of their responsibilities as citizens. When we provide talented students with knowledge and skills and give them a task, great things happen. When their task is to improve our society and democracy, ALL of us benefit. Goal #2: Provide high-impact, hands-on summer internship opportunities in public service for up to 150 Rutgers sophomores and juniors Academic research shows high-impact student practices, including internships, lead to higher graduation rates, levels of student satisfaction in the college experience,  and increased levels of career success. Student Service Stories Treyvon Watson This summer Trevyon connected the nonprofit's clients with everything from housing, food, showers and laundry facility to mental health services and case workers. Morgan Dunkel Morgan wrote and designed flyers, coordinated clothing drives and worked Pride Month events for the statewide LGBTQ+ advocacy organization. Quincy Wright Quincy was a professional exchange intern this summer at World Affairs Council of Philadelphia, working to connect global delegates with Philadelphia nonprofits and businesses to bolster the city’s international presence. Yiraldo Campos Yiraldo spent his summer as a Scarlet Service intern in the Manhattan organization’s LGBTQ Law Project, working as an interpreter and paralegal with mostly queer and transgender Latino clients seeking name changes and/or asylum. Kaiel Maynor Kaiel, a Rutgers-Newark student, worked this summer for Grow It Green Morristown, a nonprofit urban farm, that donates the food they’ve grown back to the community and also teaches children where their food comes from. Ryan Real Ryan spent much of the summer as a Scarlet Service intern at Five Loaves Food Pantry at the College Avenue Community Church in New Brunswick, where he participated in the development of dietary health programs, researched grant opportunities and updated the organization's computer system. Read More Here Help the Community, Empower Students, and Support Rutgers Scarlet Service Internships Make a gift in support of Rutgers Scarlet Service. Give Now Summer 2026 Student Information Applications for Summer 2026 are open! Deadline is Wednesday, November 19 at 11:59 pm. ** Please note that the application processes for the RSSI Program and RSS-DC program are separate. While you can only participate in one program, students are encouraged to apply to both, if interested. Learn more about the RSS-DC program here. Apply Now! Student Application Preview Accordion Content What is the Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship Program? Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program provides select students from Rutgers-Camden, Newark, and New Brunswick with an opportunity to earn up to $5,000 while interning for a minimum of 150-hours in no less than 10-12 weeks during the summer at a public service nonprofit organization or direct-service government office. These internships are primarily in-person internships, with 75-100% of the work being completed at the internship site. Students also participate in a concurrent virtual, asynchronous internship course designed to explore and engage with the topic of public service while building their career readiness and professionalism skills. Students are required to attend multiple in-person and virtual events and field trips during the Spring and Summer 2026 semesters, as well as a Celebration event in early Fall 2026. Specific dates will be announced soon. Travel to Rutgers-New Brunswick campus is necessary for the Kickoff and Celebration events. What will I learn through the concurrent RSSI summer virtual course? Course Description The RSSI course is a 3-credit, pass/no credit, virtual and asynchronous internship course that will require an estimated one (1) hour of coursework per week. This course is meant to ground students’ experience in their public service internships, providing them with the theoretical foundation and professional and civic skillset to thrive in their internship site and their communities. Through readings, reflections, and virtual discussions, students will explore and engage with the topic s of public servic e and civic engagement while building their career readiness and professionalism. Who is eligible for the program? The application process for this Program is open to Rutgers-Camden, New Brunswick, and Newark current sophomores and juniors who will have 61-91 credits completed by the end of the Spring 202 6 semester. Applicants must have an overall cumulative GPA of 2.5 or higher and be in good standing with the university. Applicants must also have the ability to travel to an internship site in New Jersey, New York City, or Philadelphia region, and have a passion for community engagement and/or public service. Students who participated in previous RSSI Program cohorts are not eligible to reapply. Can transfer students in their first semester at Rutgers apply? Transfer students in their first semester at Rutgers are eligible to apply as long as: Their transfer and Rutgers credits equal 61 credits at the end of the Spring 2026 semester They achieve a GPA of at least 2.5 in the Fall 2025 semester Can International students participate? Yes, international students eligible for Curriculum Practical Training (CPT) can apply to the RSSI Program. This includes the fulfillment of at least one academic year in the United States. You can find additional information on Curricular Practical Training (CPT) here . Can undocumented students participate? Yes, undocumented students are welcome to apply. RSSI is open to all students regardless of immigration status. Why should I apply? Stipends, experience, knowledge, scholarships, networking, and events! Stipends and Experience: Students selected for RSSI receive $5,000 for completing a minimum of 150-hour at a hands-on summer internship in public service where they make a difference, contribute to solutions to societal problems, view American life from a fresh perspective, and develop a better understanding of their responsibilities as citizens. Rutgers stipends are paid in two installments as long as students are making satisfactory progress towards internship goals. Note: Students are responsible for all applicable taxes on their $5,000 stipend. Knowledge and Scholarships: Accepted students participate in a concurrent virtual, asynchronous course designed to explore and engage with the topic of public service while building their career readiness and professionalism skills. RSSI students also receive a scholarship to cover the expense of the 3-credit course. Networking and Events: Meet talented students from across Rutgers University who have a passion for making the world a better place. Students selected for RSSI will participate in multiple events and field trips at the beginning, middle, and end of the RSSI program where they will hear from guest speakers from the world of public service and build fellowship with one another. After their summer internship experience, students will share their stories and experience at a Celebration event with the broader university community. How will I be matched with an internship for the RSSI Program? Selected students and employers will participate in virtual interviews in February 2026. After the interviews, students will rank their internship site preferences and employers will rank their student preferences. The RSSI administrators will determine summer matches based on these rankings. Most students are matched with their first or second internship site preference. Final matches between students and employers will be determined by the end of April 2026. What do I need to know about the application process? All eligible students must apply by 11:59 pm on Wednesday, November 19 . Candidates complete an application form, attach a resume and unofficial transcript, and respond to three essay prompts. The application form also asks for the name, title, and contact of two references, of which at least one should be academic-based. Student applications will be reviewed by a selection committee and RSSI administrators. All student applicants will be notified of their status in the program by mid-January 2026 . In late January 2026, selected students will be responsible for scheduling their interview slots with internship sites through RSSI's virtual interview process. Specific information about the virtual interview process will be shared in January. After the interviews, students will rank their internship site preferences and employers will rank their student preferences. The RSSI administrators will determine summer matches based on these rank ings. See above question for more information on securing an internship. Does RSSI provide travel arrangements to and from the internship site? No, students are responsible for arranging their own travel to and from the internship site. No travel allowance will be provided in addition to the $5,000 RSSI stipend. How do I contact someone to inquire about RSSI? Contact Heather Escalona Benitez , Senior Assistant Director, at rssi@echo.rutgers.edu to learn more. Application Instructions Apply Now. Before completing your application, please collect/draft the following items: resume, unofficial transcript, contact information for two references, and responses to your essay questions. All materials must be submitted via this form by Wednesday, November 19 at 11:59 pm . It is important to note that students cannot save draft version(s) of this form and return to it later. General Applicant Information General Academic Information Required Supporting Documentation: Resume: One page preferred, two pages maximum Unofficial Transcript: Rutgers transcript, including your current semester of classes. If you are transfer student and have been at Rutgers for less than one academic year, please include transcripts from previous colleges/universities. Contact Information for One Reference: Provide the name, title, organization, phone number, current workplace, and email address of one contact who can speak to your experiences and work ethic. References can include advisors, faculty, staff, supervisors, etc. Your references should not be related to you. RSSI Essay Questions Why are you applying, what do you hope to get out of RSSI, and why should you be selected? Please describe your interest in public service and how it might relate to your career goals. Applicants are encouraged to include previous relevant experience or related personal experiences. (300 words or less) Share how you plan to meet RSSI Program requirements this summer while also managing your other personal, academic, and professional priorities and responsibilities. (200 words or less) Tell us about a time you had to work with someone who had a different viewpoint than you. How did you approach the situation to come to an understanding or resolution? Reflect on how this situation may help you to intern in public service. (200 words or less) Tell us about a time you had to overcome a challenge or obstacle at school or in the workplace. Describe your approach to the situation and what motivated you to persevere. What did you learn from this experience? (200 words or less) Is there anything else we should know about you? (optional) General Internship Questions Applicant Affirmation Summer 2026 Employer Information Are you a non-profit organization with a public service mission or a direct-service government office? If you are interested in hosting an RSSI summer intern, please contact rssi-experiences@echo.rutgers.edu to connect with a member of the Career Exploration and Success team. Deadline to apply is Friday, December 19, 2025. Employers, Apply Now! Accordion Content What are the requirements to be an employer internship site? All internship sites must: Be a nonprofit with 501c3 status or a direct service government office with no affiliation to Rutgers University Have a mission in service to the public and engage in civic engagement activities Provide an internship experience that meets one of our five public service pathways Provide a primarily in-person (at least 75% in person) immersive, high-impact learning experience Host an intern for a minimum of 150 hours for no less than 10-12 weeks between May 26, 2026 and August 12, 2026 (averaging 15 hours per week) Experiences must incorporate opportunities for the intern to engage directly with the community served by the organization or indirectly through employees who can do so. How does RSSI define public service and civic engagement activities? Public service means working in partnership with community members to improve societal well-being, advocating for increased quality of life, and/or enhancing access to quality services and resources that positively impact the community. Internship experiences must belong to one of the five public service pathways , work within one or more public service sectors, and engage in civic engagement activities. Civic engagement activities focus on developing knowledge about a community, its democratic processes, and political systems while working in partnership with community members to deliberate challenges and co-create possible solutions to address and benefit the community’s needs. What type of experiences are eligible? We look to allow for a focused learning role, complete with mentorship, feedback, and the ability to ask questions. The focus should be a professional development experience – not just completing tasks. Click here for a list of the eligibility criteria and items that we typically ‘red-flag’ when reviewing applications. Rutgers departments are ineligible. Can I offer more than one internship? Yes, you can offer more than one internship for consideration. When completing the RSSI Employer Application linked at the bottom of the page, you will have the opportunity to indicate the total number of unique internship experiences and total number of vacancies you would like to offer. Can my experience be offered remotely? All internships must be primarily in-person experiences (at least 75% in person) unless the university determines a switch to remote due to health and other environmental factors. Can Rutgers Departments or Affiliated Organizations Apply? Only organizations that are external to Rutgers University are eligible to apply for RSSI. The applying organization cannot be affiliated to Rutgers in the following ways: Cannot be a Rutgers Department/Satellite/Extension/Farm/Program/Initiative – only external organizations can apply The intern’s full-time supervisor cannot be Rutgers faculty or staff The external organization cannot be receiving other sources of Rutgers funding The permanent business address and/or office cannot be owned/operated by Rutgers University The external organization does not have a reporting structure that includes Rutgers University in any capacity (funding, staff hierarchy, etc.) How can I design learning objectives for our internship experience? Follow this link to a walkthrough document that outlines learning objectives and how to create them for your internship experience. What is the timeline and process for this program? Student applications are due in November. Employer applications are due in December 2025. These applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Those selected to participate in our 2026 program will be notified by mid-January. If selected, employers will be asked for their availability for interviews held in mid-February with selected students. Selected employers will participate in virtual interviews with students. After the interviews, students will rank their internship site preferences and employers will rank their student preferences. The RSSI administrators will determine summer matches based on these rankings. Final matches between students and employers will be determined by the end of April 2026. Please see the question below regarding guaranteed acceptance or student placement. Am I guaranteed acceptance into the program and an intern? Rutgers University does not guarantee acceptance into the Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program or guarantee that all placement sites or experiences will receive a matched student. We have a limited number of employer positions available for this program, therefore, in order to facilitate appropriate matches with students, we offer more internship opportunities than students selected for the program. If you are not accepted into the program or matched with a student, please know we can assist you in gaining awareness of your organization by setting up a Rutgers Handshake profile. Please email recruiting@echo.rutgers.edu for more information. Support Excellence Make a gift in support of the Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship Program. GIVE NOW RSSI Steering Committee The Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program, run by the Rutgers-Camden Career Center, Rutgers-Newark Office of Career Resources and Exploration, and Rutgers University–New Brunswick Career Exploration and Success, consult the steering committee on decisions impacting the program. Please contact rssi@echo.rutgers.edu if you have any questions. Heather Escalona Benitez RSSI Program Lead Madeline Levy RSSI Lead, Rutgers University–Camden Jennifer Broyles RSSI Lead, Rutgers University-New Brunswick Toi Tyson RSSI Lead, Rutgers University-Newark Tamara Peters RSSI Employer Lead, Rutgers University- New Brunswick Accordion Content Site Footer College Ave Campus 106 Somerset Street, 4th Floor New Brunswick, NJ 08901 Busch Campus 604 Bartholomew Road Piscataway, NJ 08854 About CES Vision, Mission, & Values Leadership & Governance Department Structure Outcomes Data Connect Contact & Help Desk Meet with an Advisor Giving Opportunities Career & Internship Guide Follow Us Rutgers is an equal access/equal opportunity institution. Individuals with disabilities are encouraged to direct suggestions, comments, or complaints concerning any accessibility issues with Rutgers websites to accessibility@rutgers.edu or complete the Report Accessibility Barrier / Provide Feedback form. Copyright ©2026 , Rutgers, The State University of New Jersey . All rights reserved. Contact webmaster"
    },
    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/resources/careerthreads/": {
      "title": "CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire – Career Resources and Exploration",
      "description": "CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Share This: Share CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire on Facebook Share CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire on LinkedIn Share CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire on X Copy Link CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Welcome to CareerThreads, Rutgers University – Newark’s boutique career closet, where we provide students with access to professional attire at no cost, supporting their career readiness by ensuring they are equipped for interviews, internships, and other career-related opportunities. Our mission is to reduce barriers to career success by offering a wide variety of high-quality clothing and empowering students to achieve their professional goals with confidence and style. Whether you’re preparing for a job interview or your first day at work, we’re here to help you look and feel your best! Work the RUNway Clothing Drive Details We are launching the CareerThreads Professional Clothing Drive in partnership with CareerThreads , running from August through October. This initiative aims to collect professional attire for students in need. The department or organization that collects the most articles of professional clothing will be recognized at the annual Raider Career Impact Awards and featured in our first annual “ Work the RU-Nway: CareerThreads Fashion Show ”. Donation Drop-Off : We accept donation drop-offs on Fridays between 10:00 AM and 3:00 PM . Donations can be dropped off at the Office of Career Resources and Exploration located in Engelhard Hall, Suite 115 at 190 University Avenue, Newark, NJ, 07102. Appointment for Large Donations : If you have a large donation, please contact us by email at careerthreads@newark.rutgers.edu in advance to schedule a drop-off appointment. Tips for Professional Dress Schedule an Appointment Make A Donation How Does CareerThreads Work? If you are an actively enrolled student at Rutgers University – Newark, you can schedule a CareerThreads appointment through Rutgers Newark Handshake. Each appointment lasts 30 minutes. Please ensure that you arrive on time, as appointments will be canceled if you are more than 10 minutes late, and you will need to reschedule. Students can take up to five (5) clothing items from CareerThreads per semester, with a maximum of ten (10) items per academic year. The Office of Career Resources and Exploration also hosts pop-up shop events throughout the semester, where students are welcome to browse and select items, even if they have reached their semester limit. Please note: Only one student is allowed in the store at a time, and no additional guests are permitted. For any additional questions, please contact us at careerthreads@newark.rutgers.edu . Where is CareerThreads Located? What Are the Store Hours? Location CareerThreads is located within the Office of Career Resources and Exploration’s Career Coaching Suite on the First Floor of Engelhard Hall. Address : 190 University Avenue Suite 115G Hours of Operation The CareerThreads storefront is by appointment only Monday – Thursday. We conduct inventory on Fridays and do not accept appointments on this day. How Can We Donate to CareerThreads? CareerThreads equips Rutgers-Newark students with the professional attire and confidence needed for success in their careers. This initiative is made possible through the generous support of our campus and community partners, including alumni, faculty, parents, employer partners, and members of the Newark community. We welcome contributions as we continue to grow and enhance CareerThreads. For donations of six or more items, we kindly request that donors schedule an appointment. Please contact us at careerthreads@newark.rutgers.edu to arrange a drop-off. What Are Some Additional Ways to Support CareerThreads? Amazon Wishlist Want to support CareerThreads but don’t have clothing to donate? You can still help! We’ve curated a selection of professional attire and other items to enhance the inventory available for students, making it easy for you to contribute. On the CRE CareerThreads Amazon Wishlist , you choose and buy the items, and they will be shipped directly to the Office of Career Resources and Exploration! Monetary Gifts In addition to donating new or gently used professional clothing items, you may also make a financial gift to the Career Closet by clicking HERE .  Please specify “Career Resources and Exploration, CareerThreads Career Closet” in the Fund Name section to properly designate your funds. Donations We Are Seeking Interested in donating professional attire to CareerThreads ? We’d love your support! By donating professional clothing and accessories, you’re helping Rutgers University-Newark students feel confident and prepared for interviews, internships, and their first steps into their careers. Every donation—whether it’s a blazer, a pair of shoes, or a tie—makes a big impact and helps remove barriers to success. Together, we can empower our students to step into their future with confidence and style! CareerThreads accepts donations year round ! See below for the types of items we are currently seeking. PLEASE NOTE: We are in high need of the following: Accessories, hosiery, socks, and all-gendered clothing sizes Small, Medium, 2XL, Petite, Tall Professional Attire Help students dress for success! Your donation of professional clothing attire can make a world of difference for students preparing to step into their career. All-Gendered Items Blazers (solid colors or subtle patterns) Dress shirts (button-downs, solid or conservative patterns) Professional slacks (neutral colors like black, navy, gray, beige) Professional skirts (knee-length or longer, neutral colors) Sweaters and cardigans (solid colors, business-appropriate styles) Feminine Presenting Items Blouses (neutral tones or subtle prints) Professional suits (pantsuits or skirt suits) Masculine Presenting Items Suits (two- or three-piece suits in neutral tones) Accessories It’s the little details that complete the look! Donate accessories to help students feel confident and polished. Belts (black, brown, and neutral tones) Scarves (professional and lightweight styles) Briefcases and professional bags (neutral or professional colors) Professional shoes: Flats, heels, and loafers for women Oxfords, loafers, or dress shoes for men Jewelry (minimalist styles suitable for interviews) Ties (all styles and textures) Outerwear and Specialty Items From first impressions to functional style, outerwear and specialty items keep professionals ready for any weather or occasion. Professional coats and jackets (e.g., trench coats, wool coats in neutral tones) Hosiery (unopened and new) Professional head coverings (e.g., hijabs, scarves, or caps for cultural or religious purposes) New socks (neutral colors for business wear) CareerThreads Donation Policies Condition of Donated Items Clean and “Gently-Loved” (Used) : All items must be free from stains, rips, and odors. Good Condition : Items should be free from significant wear, including fading, stretching, or pilling. Current Styles : Please donate items in style within the last 3-5 years to ensure they are professional and appropriate for the current workplace environment. We also accept monetary donations . What We Cannot Accept Items that are torn, excessively worn, or damaged. Casual clothing (e.g., jeans, t-shirts, flip-flops) Items with visible logos or excessive branding. Items that require extensive cleaning, repairs, or alterations Unprofessional or inappropriate attire Donation Process Drop-Off : We accept donation drop-offs on Fridays between 10:00 AM and 3:00 PM . Donations can be dropped off at the Office of Career Resources and Exploration located in Engelhard Hall, Suite 115 at 190 University Avenue, Newark, NJ, 07102. Appointment for Large Donations : If you have a large donation, please contact us by email at careerthreads@newark.rutgers.edu in advance to schedule a drop-off appointment. Sustainability and Community Impact Eco-Friendly : We encourage donations of items that are gently used, reducing waste and promoting sustainability. Giving Back : Your donations help give Rutgers-Newark students professional attire at no cost, supporting career readiness, success, and overall confidence. Special Considerations Seasonal Donations : Please donate seasonally appropriate clothing. During the colder months, winter coats and outerwear are in higher demand and very much appreciated! Gender-Neutral and Inclusive Attire : We welcome and encourage donations of gender-neutral professional clothing and accessories to support all identities. Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/resources/raider-headshot-studio/": {
      "title": "Raider Headshot Studio",
      "description": "Raider Headshot Studio – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Raider Headshot Studio Share This: Share Raider Headshot Studio on Facebook Share Raider Headshot Studio on LinkedIn Share Raider Headshot Studio on X Copy Link The Raider Headshot Studio is a self-service professional photography studio accessible to all Rutgers University – Newark students. Schedule an Appointment Are you ready to elevate your professional presence? The Rutgers University – Newark, Office of Career Resources and Exploration (CRE) proudly presents the Raider Headshot Studio sponsored by Fiserv ! Open to all RU-N students by appointment , from Monday to Thursday, 9:30 AM to 4:30 PM, this resource provides free professional headshots to help you stand out in today’s competitive job market. Located in Engelhard Hall (Suite 101), the Raider Headshot Studio is your go-to destination for a polished and professional image. Research shows that profiles with photos receive up to 21 times more views and 9 times more connection requests on LinkedIn—don’t miss out! RU-N students can schedule their appointment today through Handshake at https://go.rutgers.edu/HSappts . Rutgers Newark faculty and staff interested in utilizing the Raider Headshot studio and learning more can contact: creheadshots@newark.rutgers.edu Take the first step toward enhancing your career prospects—visit the Raider Headshot Studio and put your best face forward! Schedule an Appointment Frequently Asked Questions How can I schedule an appointment? Login to your Rutgers Handshake account with your NetID and password. Click Career Center on the left navigation menu. Click on Appointments from your Career Center’s page, located below the school name. To request a new appointment, click the Schedule a New Appointment button in the upper-left of the page. Click on Additional Services, then click on Raider Headshot Studio. A list of available appointment dates will appear based on the category and type selected. Select the Appointment Medium from the dropdown menu (default: In-Person, Engelhard Hall, Suite 101), then enter details about the appointment in the “What can we help you with?” section. When you’re ready, click the green Request button in the lower-right corner of the screen. You’ll be taken to the appointment overview page, where you can add comments before the appointment, or cancel the appointment by clicking the red button Cancel. Who is eligible to utilize this service? Currently all eligible Rutgers University–Newark undergraduate and graduate students can utilize the Raider Headshot Studio. What happens if I do not show up to my designated appointment? We understand life happens, however, when participants fail to appear at programs, interviews, or appointments that require pre-registration, it is unfair to potential participants who wanted to attend but could not due to a waitlist. If you need to cancel, please be sure to do so with advance notice. Sample Headshots Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/channels/majors-and-careers/": {
      "title": "Majors and Careers",
      "description": "Majors and Careers – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Majors and Careers Latest News Latest News Forage Experiences Career Resources Vault Guides Videos Did you know that most careers do not require a specific major? Many students will select a major that aligns with based on their interests or abilities, which is often the same way students will select a career. This does not mean that majors and careers are the same. A major represents the academic knowledge you have developed in one field, while a career can represent many areas of knowledge, skills you have gained, and other experiences. Most recruiters and employers do not specify majors for their opportunities. They usually look for experience that shows you have the skills and qualifications related to what they need.  There are different ways to explore majors and careers. Below are a few possible approaches and resources to get you started. Selecting a major Traitify: Want to better understand your personality characteristics and career-related interests and get suggestions on careers and potential majors you can start exploring? Take advantage of Traitify, a visual personality assessment that helps you understand how your qualities and passions relate to potential careers by recommending career options based on your interests, aptitudes and education level. Access by logging into Handshake and viewing in the Resource Library. Exploring industries and RU-N majors Industry Communities, on this website: each of the Industry Community pages on this site include a list of majors at Rutgers-Newark that will give you some academic knowledge related to that industry. Many of the majors appear on more than one page, so take a look at all industries that sound interesting to you. And, remember that a major doesn’t connect directly to a career–so if you’re interested in an industry and your major isn’t listed, don’t let that prevent you from pursuing your goal! Here are links to all of the Industry Communities: Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Exploring/Multiple Interests Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Exploring career options Vault: in-depth intelligence on what it’s like to work in an industry, company, or profession—and how to position yourself to land that job (in Resources within Rutgers-Newark Handshake ) Sidebar Latest News Day in the Life: Process Manager, Environmental Sustainability 10 unique career paths for college grads pursuing sport analytics careers How to Clean Up Your Social Media Footprint Before Your Job Search How to Use Personality Type to Find a Career You Love A Day in the Life: Policy Analyst in a Nonprofit Advocacy Group (Energy) View All Career Resources NACE Career Readiness Competencies Share This: Share NACE Career Readiness Competencies on Facebook Share NACE Career Readiness Competencies on LinkedIn Share NACE Career Readiness Competencies on X Copy Link “Career readiness is a foundation from which to demonstrate requisite core competencies that broadly prepare the college educated for success … View a PDF of the NACE Competencies Rutgers-Newark Handshake Share This: Share Rutgers-Newark Handshake on Facebook Share Rutgers-Newark Handshake on LinkedIn Share Rutgers-Newark Handshake on X Copy Link Handshake helps students find the experiences they need to get career ready. From events like career fairs and workshops, to … Read More Traitify Share This: Share Traitify on Facebook Share Traitify on LinkedIn Share Traitify on X Copy Link Want to better understand your personality characteristics and career-related interests and get suggestions on careers you can start exploring? Take … Read More Entrepreneurship Concentration & Minor Share This: Share Entrepreneurship Concentration & Minor on Facebook Share Entrepreneurship Concentration & Minor on LinkedIn Share Entrepreneurship Concentration & Minor on X Copy Link Have you ever wanted to start your own business? “A Concentration in Entrepreneurship prepares students to start a business or … Read More Dream.US Career Page Share This: Share Dream.US Career Page on Facebook Share Dream.US Career Page on LinkedIn Share Dream.US Career Page on X Copy Link “Our Career Connections Program helps Scholars be career ready, make professional connections, and have opportunities to launch meaningful careers. Here … Read More Emsi Job Postings Dashboard Share This: Share Emsi Job Postings Dashboard on Facebook Share Emsi Job Postings Dashboard on LinkedIn Share Emsi Job Postings Dashboard on X Copy Link “Track job posting trends by month and compare to previous years. You can also filter by region, industry, company, job, … Read More View All Resources Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/channels/get-career-ready/": {
      "title": "Get Career Ready",
      "description": "Get Career Ready – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Get Career Ready Latest News Latest News Forage Experiences Career Resources Vault Guides Videos Your major is only one part of your career readiness process. You’ll also want to make sure you have the in-demand skills employers are looking for, and that you’ve put them to use in real-world experiences. You’ll also want to make sure these skills are clearly demonstrated on your application materials (like your resume and cover letter) and that you showcase them in interviews. Top Actions to Get Career Ready Today Know the Skills How do you really know which skills employers are looking for? The National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) set out to discover what skills college students should focus on in order to be ready for their careers. In fact, many of these are also skills you can apply right now in group projects, field experiences, and more to practice building these competencies. Get Experience Experience refers to anything you’ve engaged in that has prepared you for your professional career, whether it’s related to your major or not. You may already be in a job where you’re practicing the skills you’ll need to get you where you want to go next. If you’re not sure what you want to do, start exploring today with experiences through Forage or Parker-Dewey ! Share Your Story Whether you’re still deciding on a major or are actively searching for your next job, you’ll want to make sure to showcase your skills and experiences through an online profile. Sharing your story on Rutgers-Newark Handshake or LinkedIn is a great way to help employers find you. Be sure to include volunteer experiences, relevant courses, recent jobs, and other information about yourself. Rate your career readiness and get customized resources to help you work towards your career goals. Get started with our career readiness self-assessment today! Complete the Career Readiness Self-Assessment Latest News 5 Tips to Navigate the Transition from Campus to Career Are you ready for a change in your career? How to tell an employer you’ve changed your mind after you’ve accepted their job offer 5 Genius Ways to Better Your Career By Leveraging the Job You Already Have Top Resume Skills Employers Want to See in 2026 View All Forage Experiences Showcase your skills. Find your career fit. Forage offers bite-sized 5-6 hour virtual work experience programs that give you a genuine career advantage with Fortune 500 companies. These opportunities are completely free, with no prior experience or application needed to get started. They're a great way to try out different fields and see what you like before applying for internships or jobs! Building Your Personal Brand Experience with Ashurst Perkins Coie Ashurst Perkins Coie US Time Management and Business Communication Skills Experience with White & Case White & Case Client Service Experience with Bloomberg Bloomberg View All Experiences Career Resources LinkedIn Learning Share This: Share LinkedIn Learning on Facebook Share LinkedIn Learning on LinkedIn Share LinkedIn Learning on X Copy Link Have you heard of LinkedIn Learning? Did you know Rutgers-Newark students can access LinkedIn Learning courses for free? LinkedIn Learning … Read More Micro-Internships with Parker Dewey Share This: Share Micro-Internships with Parker Dewey on Facebook Share Micro-Internships with Parker Dewey on LinkedIn Share Micro-Internships with Parker Dewey on X Copy Link “Students from all majors can execute professional assignments to build and demonstrate skills while exploring a variety of career paths.” View Resource NACE Career Readiness Competencies Share This: Share NACE Career Readiness Competencies on Facebook Share NACE Career Readiness Competencies on LinkedIn Share NACE Career Readiness Competencies on X Copy Link “Career readiness is a foundation from which to demonstrate requisite core competencies that broadly prepare the college educated for success … View a PDF of the NACE Competencies Raider Headshot Studio Share This: Share Raider Headshot Studio on Facebook Share Raider Headshot Studio on LinkedIn Share Raider Headshot Studio on X Copy Link Take the first step toward enhancing your career prospects—visit the Raider Headshot Studio and put your best face forward! Frequently … Read More Soft Skills to Pay the Bills Share This: Share Soft Skills to Pay the Bills on Facebook Share Soft Skills to Pay the Bills on LinkedIn Share Soft Skills to Pay the Bills on X Copy Link “Skills to Pay the Bills: Mastering Soft Skills for Workplace Success,” is a curriculum developed by ODEP focused on teaching … Read More Forage Share This: Share Forage on Facebook Share Forage on LinkedIn Share Forage on X Copy Link Showcase your skills. Find your career fit.\nAt Forage, our virtual work experience programs give students the opportunity to learn … Read More View All Resources Videos Top 5 Skills Employers Look for In an Intern Video post Rutgers-Newark Handshake Tutorial Video post View All Videos Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/channels/career-ready-in-real-life/": {
      "title": "Career Ready in Real Life",
      "description": "Career Ready in Real Life – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Career Ready in Real Life Latest News Latest News Forage Experiences Career Resources Vault Guides Videos The professional world can seem like a mystery. If you’ve just gotten your first internship, on-campus job, research opportunity, fellowship, or even if you just want to know what to expect in a workplace setting, we’re here to help! Review these resources so you can feel prepared and confident navigating real-world work settings. Do Your Research The best way to know what to do and what’s expected in a work setting is to do your research. Reading blogs from reputable sources and setting your preferences to get a customized newsletter from the Career Development Center will help you stay informed and get practical tips for all career stages. This way you’ll know what employers and supervisors expect and how to make a great impression every day. Discover the Unwritten Rules There are some general standards that apply to many different fields and industries, but it’s important to know what the culture is like for your chosen field of study. Learn about your industry area so you’ll be even more prepared when you’re in a professional role. Sometimes it can feel like there’s a hidden curriculum that you might not have learned before taking a job. Keeping up with blogs and industry insights can help you get the inside scoop on what to expect! Refine Your Skills You’re never really done learning new skills to make you a valuable team member. Whether you’re looking to enhance your resume with a new technical skill, or enhance your transferable skills (like communication, problem solving, or conflict management), LinkedIn Learning can help you advance your career readiness, and it’s available free for Rutgers-Newark students. Rate your career readiness and get customized resources to help you work towards your career goals. Get started with our career readiness self-assessment today! Complete the Career Readiness Self-Assessment Latest News Changing Careers in Your 40s or 50s: A Guide for Experienced Executives How to tell an employer you’ve changed your mind after you’ve accepted their job offer How to Ask for a Raise in Your First Job: A 2026 Guide for Recent Grads Surviving your transition from student to corporate life 9 Tips to Get Career Ready View All Forage Experiences Showcase your skills. Find your career fit. Forage offers bite-sized 5-6 hour virtual work experience programs that give you a genuine career advantage with Fortune 500 companies. These opportunities are completely free, with no prior experience or application needed to get started. They're a great way to try out different fields and see what you like before applying for internships or jobs! US Time Management and Business Communication Skills Experience with White & Case White & Case View All Experiences Career Resources LinkedIn Learning Share This: Share LinkedIn Learning on Facebook Share LinkedIn Learning on LinkedIn Share LinkedIn Learning on X Copy Link Have you heard of LinkedIn Learning? Did you know Rutgers-Newark students can access LinkedIn Learning courses for free? LinkedIn Learning … Read More Guide: 5 Crucial Components for Creating an Inclusive Dress Code Share This: Share Guide: 5 Crucial Components for Creating an Inclusive Dress Code on Facebook Share Guide: 5 Crucial Components for Creating an Inclusive Dress Code on LinkedIn Share Guide: 5 Crucial Components for Creating an Inclusive Dress Code on X Copy Link View Resource The New Rules of Work: Podcast Share This: Share The New Rules of Work: Podcast on Facebook Share The New Rules of Work: Podcast on LinkedIn Share The New Rules of Work: Podcast on X Copy Link “The New Rules of Work explores the changing landscape of work and how that impacts both employees and employers. In … Read More 40 Templates to Help You Handle Your Toughest Work Emails Share This: Share 40 Templates to Help You Handle Your Toughest Work Emails on Facebook Share 40 Templates to Help You Handle Your Toughest Work Emails on LinkedIn Share 40 Templates to Help You Handle Your Toughest Work Emails on X Copy Link Email templates for just about any job searching or in-the-workplace scenario you might encounter! Get the Templates Soft Skills to Pay the Bills Share This: Share Soft Skills to Pay the Bills on Facebook Share Soft Skills to Pay the Bills on LinkedIn Share Soft Skills to Pay the Bills on X Copy Link “Skills to Pay the Bills: Mastering Soft Skills for Workplace Success,” is a curriculum developed by ODEP focused on teaching … Read More View All Resources Videos How to successfully ask for a raise at work Video post Business Etiquette Part Two: Workplace and Communications Video post View All Videos Videos How to successfully ask for a raise at work Video post Business Etiquette Part Two: Workplace and Communications Video post View All Videos Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
