    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/channels/interviewing/": {
      "title": "Interviewing",
      "description": "Interviewing – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Interviewing Latest News Latest News Forage Experiences Career Resources Vault Guides Videos You’ve been offered an interview! Now what? An interview is a chance to share who you are as a candidate and learn more about the opportunity. Whether on the phone, in person, or virtual, make sure you prepare for each stage of the interview. Interview Tips Before the Interview ► Review any application materials you have submitted. It may have been weeks (or months!) since you applied, so remind yourself how you presented your skills and qualifications. Most interviews will begin with the question “Tell me about yourself” so think about how you want to describe yourself as a candidate for the position. ► Predict questions you’ll be asked. Reread the job description, looking for key skills, requirements, and other qualifications. Review details about the organization, such as mission statement, values, key projects, and news. If you are told in advance the people who will be a part of your interview, learn more about who they are. All of these pieces of information could suggest possible questions. ► Prepare questions that you will ask. Almost all interviews will have a time for you as the candidate to ask questions. Be thoughtful about what to ask, based on your preparation. If you can find the answer through your research, don’t ask it during the interview. ► Plan your attire. If you have an in-person or virtual interview, check that all of your attire is ready (does anything need to be cleaned, polished, repaired, etc.?). If you have a phone interview, attire is still important, as dressing as if you will be seen can put you in the mindset to make a strong professional impression. ► Create your day-of plan. If you’re going to be on the phone or online for your interview, decide on a quiet space where you won’t be interrupted. If you’re interviewing in person, make sure you have a travel plan, accounting for any potential travel delays. Regardless of the type of interview, you should arrive/be ready 10-15 minutes prior to the scheduled start. During the Interview ► Be nice to everyone. Anyone you encounter during your interview, whether a formal part of the schedule or not, can share input on you as a candidate. In US culture, eye contact and handshakes are a part of these interactions. There are options for hands-free greetings; see the Hands-Free Greetings handout in Resources on this page. ► Gather your thoughts before responding. It is ok to ask for clarification, or to pause before answering a question so that you can prepare your answer. ► Bring something to take notes (and bring extra copies of your resume). You may want to collect contact information of the people you meet, or make note of information you learn during the interview. After the Interview ► Send thank you notes. An interview is an opportunity that not every candidate will get, so share your gratitude. Sidebar Latest News 9 Rookie Mistakes to Avoid When Interviewing Phone Interview Tips in 2026: How to Nail the First Screen and Move Forward 7 Questions You Should Definitely Ask When Interviewing for a Remote Job Three ways to handle job search strain Closing statement template you can use to end your interview on a high note View All Career Resources 50 Free Zoom Video Backgrounds To Look Professional On Calls Share This: Share 50 Free Zoom Video Backgrounds To Look Professional On Calls on Facebook Share 50 Free Zoom Video Backgrounds To Look Professional On Calls on LinkedIn Share 50 Free Zoom Video Backgrounds To Look Professional On Calls on X Copy Link View Resource View All Resources Vault Guides Skip filter options to posts Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Resource Categories: Advising Guidelines Applications Career Guides Hiring Policies How-tos & Examples Industry Reports Professional Writing Samples Third Party Resources Vault Guides Close Clear Filters Vault Guide to Behavioral Interviews Share This: Share Vault Guide to Behavioral Interviews on Facebook Share Vault Guide to Behavioral Interviews on LinkedIn Share Vault Guide to Behavioral Interviews on X Copy Link The Vault Guide to Behavioral Interviews contains essential tips for preparing for and acing behavioral interview questions. These questions (which … Read More Vault Guide to Finance Interviews, 9th Edition Share This: Share Vault Guide to Finance Interviews, 9th Edition on Facebook Share Vault Guide to Finance Interviews, 9th Edition on LinkedIn Share Vault Guide to Finance Interviews, 9th Edition on X Copy Link The Vault Guide to Finance Interviews, Ninth Edition is a must-have resource for anyone seeking a job with an investment … Read More View All Resources Videos In-Person Interviewing Video post View All Videos Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://careers.newark.rutgers.edu/channels/graduate-and-professional-school/": {
      "title": "Graduate and Professional School",
      "description": "Graduate and Professional School – Career Resources and Exploration | Rutgers University – Newark Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Log in Sign up Guests Search Keywords Make an Appointment Rutgers-Newark Handshake Request a Workshop Home About Us Staff CRE Student Employees Student Outcomes People We Connect Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Campus Partners Faculty Employers/Recruiters Family Industry Pathways Exploring/Multiple Interests Arts, Media, & Entertainment Data & Technology Financial Services, Logistics, & Management Government, Law, & Public Policy Healthcare, Pharmaceuticals, & Sciences Natural Resources, Sustainability, & Environmental Science Nonprofit, Social Justice, & Education Explore Opportunities Detecting Fraudulent Job Postings Experiential Opportunities Jobs and Internships Job Searching Making Connections Rutgers Scarlet Service Internship (RSSI) Program Professional Resources CareerThreads – Free Professional Attire Raider Headshot Studio Majors and Careers Get Career Ready Career Ready in Real Life Resumes and Cover Letters Interviewing Graduate and Professional School Personal Statements Videos on Demand Events All Events Fall 2026 Raider Career and Internship Fair at Rutgers University-Newark Raider Career Impact Awards Graduate and Professional School Latest News Latest News Forage Experiences Career Resources Vault Guides Videos Information: Continuing your education in graduate or professional school can be a great option if it helps to support your career goals. While graduate school may lead to a higher salary, it’s not a guarantee. Think about the costs (time, effort, money)as you decide if a graduate degree is right for you. Here are a few ways that a graduate/professional degree can support your goals: ► The degree is required for your career goal ► The degree gives you knowledge or skills that you would not learn otherwise ► The degree allows you to change career direction In some cases, the costs of attending graduate school may be covered by fellowships, stipends, scholarships, or other sources. These may be offered by the graduate program, but there may also be programs offered by employers that can provide financial support. Graduate study does not have to happen immediately after the Bachelor’s degree. In fact, there are many programs that expect or require candidates to show they have experience, through work, research, or other ways. Be sure to connect with professionals, faculty, staff, or others to learn more about your options. Learn about personal statements here . Sidebar Latest News Celebrating Vault’s Top-Ranked Firms for LGBTQ+ Equity: Armanino, Bain, McKinsey, and Schellman Making Your Way into a Graduate Program Why You Shouldn’t Use ChatGPT to Write Your College and Grad School Admissions Essays How to Ask for a Letter of Recommendation Reach Your Professional Goals and Build Your Skills…Right from Your Laptop! View All Career Resources Versatile PhD Share This: Share Versatile PhD on Facebook Share Versatile PhD on LinkedIn Share Versatile PhD on X Copy Link The Versatile PhD mission is to help graduate students, ABDs and PhDs identify, prepare for and excel in professional careers. … Read More Aurora by Beyond the Professoriate Share This: Share Aurora by Beyond the Professoriate on Facebook Share Aurora by Beyond the Professoriate on LinkedIn Share Aurora by Beyond the Professoriate on X Copy Link “AURORA is the only online professional development training platform for graduate students, postdocs, faculty, and PhDs that helps them successfully … Read More Tips for Talking to Graduate/Professional School Recruiters Share This: Share Tips for Talking to Graduate/Professional School Recruiters on Facebook Share Tips for Talking to Graduate/Professional School Recruiters on LinkedIn Share Tips for Talking to Graduate/Professional School Recruiters on X Copy Link Potential questions to ask: What are your graduates prepared to do?\nWhat areas do your grads work in?\nAre there … Read More ImaginePhD Share This: Share ImaginePhD on Facebook Share ImaginePhD on LinkedIn Share ImaginePhD on X Copy Link ImaginePhD is a free online career exploration and planning tool for PhD students and postdoctoral scholars in the humanities and … Read More myIDP Share This: Share myIDP on Facebook Share myIDP on LinkedIn Share myIDP on X Copy Link myIDP provides: Exercises to help you examine your skills, interests, and values\nA list of 20 scientific career paths with … Read More Rutgers-Newark Handshake Share This: Share Rutgers-Newark Handshake on Facebook Share Rutgers-Newark Handshake on LinkedIn Share Rutgers-Newark Handshake on X Copy Link Handshake helps students find the experiences they need to get career ready. From events like career fairs and workshops, to … Read More View All Resources Vault Guides Skip filter options to posts Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Resource Categories: Advising Guidelines Applications Career Guides Hiring Policies How-tos & Examples Industry Reports Professional Writing Samples Third Party Resources Vault Guides Close Clear Filters Vault Guide to the International MBA Job Search Share This: Share Vault Guide to the International MBA Job Search on Facebook Share Vault Guide to the International MBA Job Search on LinkedIn Share Vault Guide to the International MBA Job Search on X Copy Link The Vault Guide to the International MBA Job Search contains essential U.S. job-search tips for international students, covering branding, networking, … Read More Vault Guide to the First Year of Law School Share This: Share Vault Guide to the First Year of Law School on Facebook Share Vault Guide to the First Year of Law School on LinkedIn Share Vault Guide to the First Year of Law School on X Copy Link The Vault Guide to the First Year of Law School offers advice for new law students on surviving law school, … Read More Vault Guide to Navigating Your Legal Career in the Time of Coronavirus Share This: Share Vault Guide to Navigating Your Legal Career in the Time of Coronavirus on Facebook Share Vault Guide to Navigating Your Legal Career in the Time of Coronavirus on LinkedIn Share Vault Guide to Navigating Your Legal Career in the Time of Coronavirus on X Copy Link The Vault Guide to Navigating Your Legal Career in the Time of Coronavirus provides expert advice to help law students … Read More Practice Perspectives: Vault’s Guide to Legal Practice Areas, 2019 Edition Share This: Share Practice Perspectives: Vault’s Guide to Legal Practice Areas, 2019 Edition on Facebook Share Practice Perspectives: Vault’s Guide to Legal Practice Areas, 2019 Edition on LinkedIn Share Practice Perspectives: Vault’s Guide to Legal Practice Areas, 2019 Edition on X Copy Link Practice Perspectives: Vault’s Guide to Legal Practice Areas is the insider’s guide to what it’s really like to practice at … Read More View All Resources Facebook Instagram X (formerly Twitter) runsa@newark.rutgers.edu CARE Team © 2026 Rutgers University - Newark  |  All Rights Reserved Universitywide New Brunswick Camden RBHS MyRutgers Blackboard ScarletMail People Search Rutgers, the State University of New Jersey, is an equal opportunity, affirmative action institution Privacy Policy | Terms of Service"
